  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "id": "span-style-font-family-charter-georgia-cambria-quot-times-new-roman-quot-serif-font-size-21px-font-weight-400-letter-spacing-0-003em-span",
      "title": "<span style=\"font-family: Charter, Georgia, Cambria, &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif; font-size: 21px; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"></span>",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">When you see a&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">recursive algorithm</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, you may wonder:</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>“How long will it take?”</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Since the function&nbsp;<strong>calls itself on smaller inputs</strong>, its time depends on those smaller calls.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A&nbsp;<strong>recurrence relation</strong>&nbsp;just describes this dependency.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-what-is-a-recurrence-relation",
      "title": "1. What Is a Recurrence Relation?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A recurrence relation is just a&nbsp;<strong>formula that describes itself using smaller versions of itself.</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In plain English:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<em>To find the answer for size n, use the answer for something smaller than n.</em>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p>Example&nbsp;<strong>climbing stairs.&nbsp;</strong></p><p>If you want to reach step&nbsp;<strong>n</strong>, you could have come from:</p><ul><li>Step n−1 (took one step), or</li><li>Step n−2 (took two steps)</li></ul><p>So the number of ways to reach step n is:</p><p></p><p><strong>Ways(n) = Ways(n−1) + Ways(n−2)</strong></p><p></p><p>with base cases: Ways(1) = 1, Ways(2) = 2</p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "That's a recurrence relation. Simple as that.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-why-do-we-need-this",
      "title": "2. Why Do We Need This?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When you write a recursive function, it calls itself on&nbsp;<strong>smaller inputs.</strong>&nbsp;That behavior has a cost and recurrence relations help us&nbsp;<strong>measure that cost.</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p>Example&nbsp;<strong>Binary Search</strong>:</p><p>Every time Binary Search runs, it:</p><ul><li>Cuts the array in half</li><li>Searches only one half</li><li>Does a quick comparison (constant work)</li></ul><p>That translates to:</p><p></p><p><strong>T(n) = T(n/2) + O(1)</strong></p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Solving this tells us Binary Search runs in&nbsp;<strong>O(log n)</strong>&nbsp;which matches what we already know.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Recurrence relations are simply&nbsp;<strong>the math behind recursion.</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-the-general-pattern",
      "title": "3. The General Pattern",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Most recurrence relations you'll see look like this:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<strong>T(n) = a × T(n/b) + f(n)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Where:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong>a</strong>&nbsp;= how many recursive calls are made",
            "<strong>n/b</strong>&nbsp;= size of each subproblem",
            "<strong>f(n)</strong>&nbsp;= extra work done outside the recursive calls"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Here are some common examples:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Notice something cool&nbsp;<strong>every level does exactly n total work.</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "How many levels are there? The tree keeps halving until it reaches size 1, so there are&nbsp;<strong>log n levels.</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Total work = n × log n =&nbsp;<strong>O(n log n)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "That's the power of drawing the recursion tree it turns math into a picture.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-how-to-actually-solve-recurrences",
      "title": "4. How to Actually Solve Recurrences",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Writing the recurrence is the easy part.&nbsp;<strong>Solving it</strong>&nbsp;finding the actual Big-O is where the real work is.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "There are four main techniques. Let's go through each one simply.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "(a) Substitution Method (Guess and Verify)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You make an&nbsp;<strong>educated guess</strong>&nbsp;about the answer, then&nbsp;<strong>prove it's correct</strong>&nbsp;using math.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><strong>Example:</strong>&nbsp;T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n</p><p><strong>Step 1: Make a guess:</strong>&nbsp;This looks like Merge Sort, so we guess&nbsp;<strong>T(n) = O(n log n).</strong></p><p><strong>Step 2: &nbsp;Prove it:</strong>&nbsp;We want to show T(n) ≤ c × n log n for some constant c.</p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Plug in the guess:&nbsp;</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Step by step:</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"></span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Assume:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "T(n) ≤ c · n log n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "SubstituteT(n/2)T(n/2):",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "T(n) ≤ 2 × c(n/2)log(n/2) + n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Simplify:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "= cn(log n − 1) + n\n= cn log n − cn + n\n= cn log n − n(c − 1)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Since&nbsp;<strong>c &gt; 1</strong>, the last term is negative, so:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "T(n) ≤ cn log n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">For any c &gt; 1, this is ≤ cn log n.&nbsp;</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<strong>Guess confirmed → T(n) = O(n log n)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Think of it like a detective you form a hypothesis, then gather evidence to back it up.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "(b) Recursion Tree Method ( Draw It Out )",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If you're a visual thinker, this method is your best friend. You&nbsp;<strong>draw the recursion as a tree</strong>&nbsp;and add up the work at each level.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><strong>Example:</strong>&nbsp;T(n) = 3T(n/3) + n</p><p>Imagine you're a manager who splits a project into 3 equal parts every time:</p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785249029711-591864560.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every level does&nbsp;<strong>n total work.</strong>&nbsp;Number of levels =&nbsp;<strong>log₃ n</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Total = n × log n =&nbsp;<strong>O(n log n)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Same result, no algebra needed just drawing.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "(c) Master Theorem (The Shortcut)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When you see&nbsp;<strong>T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n)</strong>, you can skip all the work and jump straight to the answer using the&nbsp;<strong>Master Theorem.</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It's a ready-made formula for divide-and-conquer recurrences.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<i>We'll cover this in detail in the next chapter it's powerful enough to deserve its own spotlight.</i>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "(d) Iterative Expansion &nbsp;(Unroll and Spot the Pattern)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When nothing else is obvious, just&nbsp;<strong>keep expanding the recurrence</strong>&nbsp;until a pattern appears.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><strong>Example:</strong>&nbsp;T(n) = T(n−1) + n</p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Start with:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "T(n) = T(n−1) + n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Expand step by step:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "T(n) = T(n−2) + (n−1) + n\n     = T(n−3) + (n−2) + (n−1) + n\n     = ...\n     = T(1) + 2 + 3 + ... + n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now the sum:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "2 + 3 + ... + n ≈ n(n+1)/2"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So,",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "T(n) = Θ(n²)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is like peeling an onion one layer at a time:&nbsp;<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">This is just the sum 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n =&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n(n+1)/2</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "So T(n) =&nbsp;<strong>O(n²)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Simple loops disguised as recursion often turn out to be O(n²) and now you can prove it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": []
}
